  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 PM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThe forecast generally still looks to be on track for the short \nterm, with strong gusty winds and some light higher terrain snow \naccumulations the primary concerns. \n\nThe threat for gusty winds still looks similar to the previous \nforecast cycle. Strong and gusty winds are expected to continue into \ntomorrow across the entire area. The strongest gusts are expected \nmainly east of I-75 across portions of the central and northern \nvalley, the east TN mountains and southwest VA. A Wind Advisory \nremains in place for these areas and wind gusts from 30 up to 40 mph \nare expected. Areas not in the wind advisory are still expected to \nsee gusts from 20 to 30+ mph at times.\n\nAs for the precipitation, the rain has been changing to snow on \nschedule and in general agreement with latest REFS guidance. Latest \nREFS and other hi-res model guidance still indicate that some of the \nhigher terrain of the Plateau, SW VA, and the E TN/SW NC mountains \nwill see light snow accumulations tonight into Friday morning, with \nmost locations that see any accumulations receiving around an inch \nor less although a few higher mountains may get into the 2 to 3 inch \nrange. Many valley areas will likely see a changeover to snow or at \nleast a mix as well tonight, but the warm ground and temperatures \nbottoming out near or slightly above freezing should inhibit any \naccumulations there. Given all of this, have continued with previous \nshifts thinking and will have no winter weather advisory due to the \nlow probability of even minor impacts. Will continue to highlight \nthe snow with an SPS.\n\nMost areas should be precip free by mid morning Friday. However, \nsome lingering light precip is expected to occur across northeast TN \nand southwest VA through Friday. Saturday will be dry with \nseasonable temperatures.\n\nAnother system will likely bring rain Sunday into Sunday night. Wind \nfields look likely to be strong with this system and direction may \nbe favorable for mountain wave enhancement, so strong gusty winds \nwill be possible especially across the higher elevations and \nfoothills of the mountains. There may be enough instability for a \nfew rumbles of thunder Sunday as well.  Wind shear will likely be \nsignificant, but right now ensemble data suggests convective energy \nis likely to be minimal. This will bear watching, but right now the \nminimal convective energy would suggest little or no severe storm \nthreat.  \n\nAfter we clear out on Monday, the remainder of the forecast through \nThursday looks mainly dry. Monday will be on the cool side, but then \na warming trend can be expected for Tuesday through Thursday.",
